---
/**
 * Cookie Policy. Draft. Needs a human read before launch.
 *
 * This site sets no cookies. That claim is the whole point of the page, so it
 * has to stay true: adding analytics, an embed, or any third party script
 * means rewriting this page in the same commit. There is deliberately no
 * consent banner, because a banner on a site that sets nothing is theatre.
 */
import Legal from "../layouts/Legal.astro";
import { business } from "../config/business";

const { form } = business.processors;
---

<Legal
  title={`Cookie policy: ${business.name}`}
  description="This site sets no cookies and loads no third party scripts, so there is no consent banner. Here is what we checked and what we found."
  path="/cookies"
  heading="Cookie policy"
  standfirst="This site does not set cookies. That is the entire policy. The rest of this page explains what we checked so you can hold us to it."
>
  <h2>What we found</h2>
  <p>
    We loaded every page of this site with a clean browser and looked at what
    was stored afterwards. The result: no cookies, no local storage, no session
    storage, nothing in an indexed database. Nothing about you is kept on your
    device by this website.
  </p>

  <h2>Why there is no banner asking you to accept cookies</h2>
  <p>
    Because there is nothing to accept. A consent banner on a site that stores
    nothing is decoration, and clicking it would achieve exactly what not
    clicking it achieves. So we left it off.
  </p>
  <p>
    If we ever add something that does set a cookie, we will build a real
    consent mechanism that blocks the thing until you say yes, rather than a
    banner that does nothing. And this page will stop saying what it says now.
  </p>

  <h2>What the site does load</h2>
  <p>
    Everything this site needs comes from this website and nowhere else. When
    you open a page your browser asks us for the page, one stylesheet, one font
    file and an icon. There is no request to any other company.
  </p>
  <p>That means, specifically:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>No analytics of any kind. We cannot tell you how many people read this.</li>
    <li>No advertising pixel. We run Meta ads for clients and have not put one on our own site.</li>
    <li>No web fonts from Google. The typeface is served from this website.</li>
    <li>No embedded video, no embedded map, no chat widget, no social buttons.</li>
    <li>No session recording and no heatmaps.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The one exception, and it is your choice</h2>
  <p>
    If you fill in the contact form and press send, your message is posted to
    {" "}{form.name}, who {form.role}. That is one request to another company,
    it only happens because you pressed the button, and it does not put a
    cookie on your device for this site. What {form.name} stores at their end
    is covered by their own privacy policy, linked from our
    {" "}<a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read the whole site, never touch the form, and no other company is involved
    at any point.
  </p>

  <h2>Your browser settings</h2>
  <p>
    You do not need to change anything to read this site privately. If you
    block cookies entirely, everything here still works, including the form.
  </p>

  <h2>If this page changes</h2>
  <p>
    The date at the top changes with it. This is the page most likely to go
    quietly out of date, so it is worth saying plainly: if you find something
    on this site setting a cookie, we have made a mistake. Email
    {" "}<a href={`mailto:${business.email}`}>{business.email}</a> and we will
    fix it.
  </p>
</Legal>
